**Chapter 10, Section 18: "I Had a Dream"**

――The moment that being alighted upon the street, breath stopped.

Not the breath of one person, but the breath of the world itself.

Once, when he had the chance to speak with the strongest of the Volakian Empire, he told Garfiel—who was always eager to know the convictions that supported such transcendent strength—the following:

"To put it simply, the world is a stage! The light of the sun, the sound of the wind, the scent of the earth, the richness of greenery—all of it is nothing more than staging to make the actors shine. Then, why would the world go so far as to adorn and lavishly dress the actors? It is because the world itself is the greatest, most enthusiastic fan, whose expectations are fired up by the actors' performances, their brilliant lines, and their unforgettable scenes!"

That philosophy was so far outside common sense that, frankly, it was quite difficult to understand.

But in this moment, and only this moment, he felt he could grasp a part of the logic that the "Blue Lightning" had so bombastically proclaimed. ――The idea that the world watches people as if enjoying a play.

And that is precisely why, now, he could sense so clearly that the world had stopped breathing.

"――――"

Behind Garfiel and the others, who stood with their backs to the high wall separating the nobles' district from the commoners' district, a lone man blocked the road ahead. He had flaming red hair and sky-blue eyes.

He didn't just stand there; he barred the way. ――Yes, the man stood in their path.

"Is that you, Reinhard-san? ...You're the worst pursuer I could possibly imagine."

"I feel the same way, Otto. I regret that it's come to this."

The first to recover his sense of self from the shock of astonishment was Otto, who truly possessed the strongest nerves in the camp.

In response to Otto's murmur, a statement in which even he couldn't fully suppress his emotion, the man—the "Sword Saint," Reinhard van Astrea—did not deny the description of being a pursuer.

With his hand resting on the hilt of the "Dragon Sword" at his waist, he narrowed his sky-blue eyes slightly.

"I have received orders from the royal castle for your capture. I ask that you surrender."

"And if we refuse, by force?"

"That is what will happen. It's not my true intention... no, my personal feelings are irrelevant."

"Indeed. What matters is not the place of one's heart, but the results of one's actions."

As he spoke, Otto gradually heightened the tension throughout his body. Reinhard, on the other hand, showed no change in the air about him from the moment he had appeared.

He had already decided how he would act. And once he had decided, he would see that will through to the end—that is what it means to be the "Sword Saint."

"Reinhard-san, was it? You said you received orders from the royal castle. Do you know what has become of Sister-sama and Emilia-san?"

Rem voiced the question from diagonally behind Garfiel.

Garfiel and Otto had stepped forward, and Rem stood behind them, sheltering Petra and Meili—who had drawn close to each other in an instant—with her arms, as she bravely fixed her gaze on Reinhard. The answer to Rem's question was something Garfiel also wanted to know. He suspected that Otto, who had immediately decided to leave the government office, had anticipated this situation.

"Unfortunately, I did not see either of them within the castle. However, I am aware of the circumstances under which they were summoned. In all likelihood, they are being held in custody."

"Have they been treated roughly..."

"They have not. At least, I can guarantee that no such conduct has come from the Kingdom's soldiers. Both Emilia-sama and Ram-san should have the judgment not to cause a commotion recklessly."

"...I see. Thank you for answering."

To Rem, who didn't forget to add that slight venom even after receiving information that was, for Garfiel and the others, somewhat reassuring, Reinhard closed one eye.

But the truth lies in what Otto said. What's important is not sentiment, but action.

"Reinhard-san, this situation has clearly been orchestrated. The suspicions against Natsuki-san and Crusch-sama, the containment of our Emilia camp... Ferris-sama isn't in a safe position, either. Even so—"

"――I am the 'Sword Saint.'"

"――――"

"I bear the duty of being the Kingdom's sword, and I will fulfill the role I have been tasked with. I warn you again. Please surrender. Even if you are taken to the royal castle, you will not be treated poorly. I will do everything I can to—"

"How much weight do your words hold, standing here before us now?"

Otto's resolute answer pierced through Reinhard's call for surrender, delivered with a steady expression and tone of voice.

That was the consensus not just of Otto, but of all five, including Garfiel.

No matter how Reinhard tried to phrase it, he stood there blocking their path.

"...It seems I cannot become friends with you."

"No. I had a feeling it would be like this."

That was the conclusion of the discussion between Otto and Reinhard, meant to sway the other's thinking.

Strangely enough, they both came to the same conclusion, as if in perfect sync—that further words were meaningless.

In fact, Otto's judgment was accurate.

Now that Reinhard had caught up, the royal castle side already knew that Garfiel and the others were here. If the number of pursuers increased, their escape route would vanish, and if even that Marcos were to make a move, that possibility would narrow further.

"No other soldiers are coming. A perimeter has been ordered, but I am the only one here."

